You cannot find a vulnerability with ffuf directly because it is a fuzzer, not a scanner. Instead, you find anomalous behaviors, unexpected status codes, or unlinked assets that expose flaws.
By analyzing the variations in responses, you can discover five distinct categories of bugs.
------------------------------
## 1. High-Severity Information Disclosures & Hidden Assets
This is the most common category of bug uncovered via path and directory fuzzing.

* Exposed Environment Files (.env, config.php.bak): Gives you raw access to database credentials, API secret keys, and cloud infrastructure passwords.
* Hidden Git Repositories (/.git/HEAD): Allows you to download the source code of the entire application using tools like git-dumper.
* Exposed Administrative Panels (/admin-v2, /stage-login): Bypasses obscurity protections and points you straight to weak or default login endpoints.
* Information Leakage via Stack Traces: Fuzzing bad characters or unexpected inputs can force the app to return a 500 Internal Server Error, exposing database queries or internal file paths.

## 2. Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA / IDOR)
Discovered by fuzzing API endpoints that contain user-specific identifiers (like user IDs or UUIDs) in the URL path.

* Horizontal IDOR: Fuzzing an account identifier (/api/v1/users/FUZZ/profile) allows you to view or edit the profile data of other users on the platform.
* Vertical Privilege Escalation: Fuzzing an administrative endpoint with a standard user session cookie (/api/v1/admin/users/FUZZ/delete) can allow a regular user to perform high-privilege actions.

## 3. Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) & Remote File Inclusion (RFI)
Discovered by fuzzing parameter values that handle external URLs, or by fuzzing unlinked parameters entirely.

* Internal Network Scanning: If an endpoint accepts a URL parameter, fuzzing the value with internal IP ranges (/redirect?url=http://127.0.0.1:FUZZ) can allow you to map out restricted internal ports.
* Unlinked Parameter Injection: Fuzzing GET/POST keys on a feature that handles webhooks or external data can uncover hidden parameters that force the backend server to make outward requests to your server.

## 4. Mass Assignment & Business Logic Flaws
Discovered by fuzzing the parameters sent inside the JSON or URL-encoded body of a POST/PUT request.

* Privilege Squatting: When registering or updating a profile, fuzzing additional parameter names in the JSON body (such as "is_admin": true or "role": "superuser") can force the backend to assign you higher privileges if the parameters are not properly validated.
* Coupon / Discount Brute Forcing: Fuzzing coupon code parameters (/checkout?coupon=FUZZ) with list arrays can allow you to guess active merchant discount configurations.

## 5. Access Control Bypass via Non-Standard Methods
Discovered by fuzzing the HTTP Request Methods or routing headers rather than the URL path.

* HTTP Method Override: If a specific path blocks a POST request, fuzzing the HTTP Verb (-X FUZZ) with PUT, PATCH, or HEAD might bypass the access control filter while still executing the underlying logic.
* Header-Based Authentication Bypass: Fuzzing non-standard request headers (such as X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1 or X-Custom-IP-Authorization: FUZZ) can trick poorly configured reverse proxies into thinking your traffic is coming from a trusted internal source.

